<p>A court in Seoul sentenced former South Korean President Yoon Suk Yeol to life in prison, finding him guilty of leading an insurrection tied to his short-lived declaration of martial law in December 2024. Judges said declaring martial law did not automatically amount to insurrection. But they ruled that Yoon’s actions—especially sending troops—were meant to obstruct the National Assembly’s ability to function, which the court treated as insurrectionary conduct. Prosecutors had sought the death penalty, arguing the move threatened South Korea’s democracy. Still, multiple outlets said analysts largely expected a life sentence, in part because the attempt was poorly planned and did not result in casualties. Yoon, who was impeached after the episode, has denied wrongdoing and says he acted within presidential authority to declare martial law, citing political gridlock and alleged threats from pro-Pyongyang forces within the opposition. The verdict closes one of South Korea’s biggest criminal trials in decades and reinforces a central message of the case: even top leaders can be held to account through courts and constitutional institutions—something some observers see as a constructive sign for the country’s democracy, even as polarization persists.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/nAJREsnI21CIKiOj11n5EUS2Az72ncVRn8dg_GXEJSmBy5AjAR0m-4-_sG6DRpRO4fNx0nxDoE1AvlvSelVRpyCELemm1nVObd6CBzId-BvbnZdN_H9NbUfg6PsEz_9pyrKmUbrNMUX6vGtir76VqHrDKPvYktSA1Pd4iot6Rrzo0JOEtoVQa4l-f8rekkswE4wOwEYTN8ioPdy5oxgCuVesFR8' alt='Former South Korean President Yoon Suk Yeol during the sentencing proceedings in Seoul.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Other sentences: The court also sentenced former Defense Minister Kim Yong-hyun to 30 years in prison, along with additional prison terms for other former military and police officials involved in enforcing the martial law decree.</li><li>Earlier conviction: In January, Yoon received a five-year sentence for other crimes linked to the martial law attempt, according to The Week’s summary of the case.</li><li>Death penalty context: South Korea has not executed anyone on death row since 1997, even though prosecutors sought capital punishment in Yoon’s case.</li><li>Court’s reasoning: The presiding judge said it was &quot;difficult to deny&quot; that Yoon meant to disable parliament’s functioning by sending troops.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Prosecutors: Prosecutors asked for the death penalty, arguing Yoon’s actions threatened South Korea’s democracy and deserved the harshest punishment. (<a href='https://theweek.com/world-news/yoon-korea-martial-law-president-life-sentence'>The Week</a>)</li><li>Yoon Suk Yeol: Yoon denied the charges and said he acted within presidential powers to declare martial law, pointing to legislative gridlock and alleged pro-Pyongyang plotting in the opposition. (<a href='https://www.rt.com/news/632801-south-korean-president-life-prison/'>RT</a>)</li><li>Yoon’s legal team: His lawyers called the verdict rigged and said they would consult with Yoon before deciding whether to appeal. (<a href='https://theweek.com/world-news/yoon-korea-martial-law-president-life-sentence'>The Week</a>)</li><li>PBS NewsHour report: PBS described the ruling as a &quot;bright spot&quot; for South Korea’s young democracy, arguing that a few tumultuous hours put democratic institutions to the test. (<a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/show/former-south-korean-president-sentenced-to-life-in-prison-for-imposing-martial-law'>PBS NewsHour</a>)</li><li>Political analysts (as summarized): Analysts largely expected a life sentence rather than execution because the power grab was poorly planned and did not lead to casualties. (<a href='https://theweek.com/world-news/yoon-korea-martial-law-president-life-sentence'>The Week</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.rt.com/news/632801-south-korean-president-life-prison/'>Former South Korean president sentenced to life in prison</a>